  "text": "Skip to the instrument\nLIFECYCLE / MUSIC BOX\nEXPERIMENT 064 · PR #127\nScore notes ↘\n\nONE CHANGE, TWO ENDINGS\n\nCleared isn’t\nfinished.\n\nSix semantic caches go quiet before project release.\nIn #127, the backend waits for the last note.\n\n01\nClearing moves from TypeScript to core.\n02\nA failed refresh keeps the old cache turn.\n03\nRelease now awaits—and carries—project failure.\n\nMusical analogy. Horizontal distance is selected event order, never elapsed time. A tail means an unfinished call; pitch identifies an owner. Six sequential, synchronous clears are arranged as one chord. Sound is optional.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT\n\nFollow the release boundary.\n#126 BASE → #127 HEAD\nRelease, held open\nRelease, then reject\nRelease, synchronous\nRefresh succeeds\nRefresh rejects\nOld answer lands late\n\nListen for a finished backend over a still-held project note in #126. In #127, both stay pending together.\n\nInjected: the configured project returns a held promise after its real synchronous cleanup ran. A direct backend query refills the definition cache during that hold.\n\n▶ Play\n↤\n←\n→\nSound\noff\nPace\n84\nListen to\n#127 head\n#126 base\nBoth · base L / head R\nSOLO A BOUNDARY\nFull ensemble\nRelease completion\nCache lifetime\nSuccessful turn\nAll owners visible and audible\n#126\nBASE\nservice clears Maps\nready\nBackend\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nQuery service\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nCache lifetime\nTYPESCRIPT · SIX MAPS\nProject graph\nCORE\nConfigured project\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nInferred project\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nProbe / Node\nPROBE / NODE\n01\n02\n03\n04\n05\n06\n07\n08\n09\n10\n11\n12\n13\n14\n15\nrelease\nrelease\nclear ×6\nrelease\nrelease\nreturn\nsettle\nmiss\nstore\nresolve gate\nsettle\nrelease\nreturn\nsettle\nhit\n#127\nHEAD\ncore owns clearing\nready\nBackend\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nQuery service\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nCache lifetime\nCORE · SIX HANDLES\nProject graph\nCORE\nConfigured project\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nInferred project\nBACKEND–TYPESCRIPT\nProbe / Node\nPROBE / NODE\n01\n02\n03\n04\n05\n06\n07\n08\n09\n10\n11\n12\n13\n14\n15\nrelease\nrelease\nclear ×6\nrelease\nrelease\nmiss\nstore\nresolve gate\nsettle\nrelease\nreturn\nsettle\nsettle\nsettle\nhit\nSTEP\n01 / 15\nJump to the split ↗\nAT THE PLAYHEAD\nSilent mode · every sound has a visible equivalent\n#126 BASE · 1/15\n\n·Backend release starts.\n\nRelease has not reached the project graph.\nDefinition cache: old entry · backend pending / graph not called\n\n#127 HEAD · 1/15\n\n·Backend release starts.\n\nRelease has not reached the project graph.\nDefinition cache: old entry · backend pending / graph not called\n\nstart / owner’s pitch\nfinish / short chime\nunfinished / held tone\n⠿\nclear ×6 / six-note chord\n×\nrejection / low buzz\ncore\nTypeScript\n≋ both pending / ↟ awaiting backend: open fifth   /   ⋈ backend done, graph pending: semitone clash   /   = aligned completion (including rejection): resolved chord\n\nSelected notes from real instrumented methods. Each build has its own event order; equal-numbered columns do not assert simultaneous execution. Query notes show the definition cache; the clearing chord covers all six. Click a note for its exact receipt.\n\nRead every selected step as text\n\nTHE COMPLETE SCORE NOTES\n\nWhat the notes mean.\n\nThis is the complete stopping layer. Every decision and surprise in the optional source depth is named here. Stated means a reason was found; unexplained means it was not found in the supplied PR, commits or relevant spec.\n\n01\nOne lifetime owner. Six separate voices.\n\nThe TypeScript service still owns its instance and query algorithms; core now supplies TurnScopedCacheScope and six isolated typed handles. Key spaces and cached identities stay separate. No package dependency direction changes. Five identity stores hold promises; position storage holds locations.\n\nStated — lifetime belongs in core; independent key/value spaces need separate handles. Preserving algorithms and identities is the PR’s stated scope.\n\nSolo + inspect ↗\n02\nThe clearing chord comes after a successful refresh.\n\nSource cache → project graph (workspace coverage only) → workspace state → begin turn. A failed state refresh skips the chord and keeps the old entry, even though earlier refresh work has run. This order is preserved; the service now receives readonly files instead of the full snapshot.\n\nStated — failed refresh must preserve the current successful turn. Unexplained — why narrow the argument.\n\nSolo + inspect ↗\n03\nClearing is immediate. Completion now waits.\n\nBoth versions clear synchronously before project release. #127 adds an await in service and backend, carrying project rejection to the backend caller. #126 can fulfill while the graph is pending and leave its rejection unhandled. Today’s concrete cleanup is synchronous; our long wait is injected. The graph already releases projects sequentially and stops at the first failure.\n\nStated — old semantics must be unavailable during pending or rejecting release. Unexplained — rationale for changed completion/failure behavior: the PR names the awaited boundary while claiming failure behavior is preserved.\n\nSolo + inspect ↗\n04\nSilencing the entries does not stop the players.\n\nBoth lifecycle methods synchronously call the same private clear. Handles remain reusable: a direct query can refill during pending release, and that entry survives completion. Clearing does not cancel old promises; a late answer cannot replace a new entry because the value is stored at creation, not settlement.\n\nUnexplained — no specific rationale for two verbs over one clear, reusable handles, or the late-settlement policy. These are source/test contracts; the refill and late-answer recordings agree with them.\n\nSolo + inspect ↗\n05\nA quiet result still counts as a result.\n\nMap.has makes cached undefined valid. Exact factory values—including rejected promises—remain cached for the turn. A synchronous factory throw stores nothing and retries. Reference and position projections still rebuild public results from cached locations; a cached entry does not mean every returned wrapper shares identity.\n\nStated — preserve valid undefined values and existing query/failure behavior. Unexplained — the original reasons for the projection and error-policy distinctions.\n\nInspect source ↗\n06\nA small public instrument, newly pinned.\n\nCore exports the scope and typed getOrCreate handle; lifecycle methods return void, while service release now returns a promise. Six service tests and four core tests are added; the five old service cases and their helpers are unchanged. The release test uses a held fake graph, and later-project skipping is asserted through that fake. This is bounded evidence, not full daemon parity.\n\nStated — commit subjects characterize identities and specify lifecycle/awaited release. Unexplained — why this public API shape or this exact test selection. No removed or weakened cases in the two changed suites.\n\nInspect source ↗\n↑ Return to the instrument\n\nRECEIPTS, NOT A SIMULATED RUNTIME\n\nRecorded methods.\nArranged music.\n\nThe browser replays a selected score; it does not run symnav. Recordings wrap the actual compiled base/head methods, observe promises and Map operations, and use a temporary TypeScript fixture. Delays and failures are explicitly injected. Pitch, tempo, grouping and “harmony” are our explanatory choices.\n\nOpen the frozen source book ↗ · Recording hashes & test inventory ↗ · Focused test output ↗ · Experiment README ↗\n\n064 / LIFECYCLE MUSIC BOX\nRead-only · no saved input\nBack to top ↑",
